   "source": [
    "```{admonition} Exercise 7\n",
    ":class: exercise\n",
    "Why does the optimization finish after 8 steps? \n",
    "```"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "editable": true,
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": ""
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "### Visualization if the optimization procedure"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "editable": true,
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": ""
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "Now we want to visualize how the geometry is changed during the optimization procedure. Ler's save the coordinates first:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 11,
   "metadata": {
    "editable": true,
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": ""
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "optimized_geos = readXYZ('geoms.xyz')\n",
    "coordinates =  read_coordinates('geoms.xyz')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "editable": true,
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": ""
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "We read in the coordinates as numpy arrays and as xyz files for visualization.\n",
    "You can use the little helper function `angle_distances` to calculate the angle and O-H distances for all 8 optimization steps. \n",
    "\n",
    "The first column of the array is the H-O-H angle, the second column and third column are the OH distances. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "metadata": {
    "editable": true,
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": ""
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "array([[  91.08777297,    1.31304226,    1.31304226],\n",
       "       [ 101.63785801,    0.94525415,    0.94525415],\n",
       "       [ 118.66796732,    0.96548737,    0.96548737],\n",
       "       [ 115.26476781,    0.92759183,    0.92759183],\n",
       "       [ 111.99755756,    0.95052782,    0.95052782],\n",
       "       [ 111.50239284,    0.94956489,    0.94956489],\n",
       "       [ 111.50239284,    0.94956489,    0.94956489]])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 12,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "angle_dist = []\n",
    "for coords in coordinates: # iterate over the list of coordinates, coords being a single point calculation result\n",
    "    angle_dist.append(angle_distances(coords))\n",
    "\n",
    "h2o_angles_dist = np.array(angle_dist[1:])\n",
    "h2o_angles_dist"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "editable": true,
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": ""
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "Let's also extract the energies after each SCF cycle. We can use grep for this"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "metadata": {
    "editable": true,
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": ""
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "[-75.76713800853429,\n",
       " -75.85604058723061,\n",
       " -75.9823812832021,\n",
       " -75.98328017098966,\n",
       " -75.98412349520544,\n",
       " -75.985335533284,\n",
       " -75.98534371689787]"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 13,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "geo_opt_energies = !grep -a '@DF-UHF Final Energy' h2o-opt.log\n",
    "geo_opt_energies = [float(x.replace('  @DF-UHF Final Energy:   ',''))  for x in geo_opt_energies] # this is some formatting to get the values from the text file\n",
    "geo_opt_energies"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "editable": true,
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": ""
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "To help you visualizing the result, we have prepared a full potential energy surface of water at the HF/6-31G level. We will load it and the plot the path our geometry optimization takes based on the angle and the distance we extracted from the trajectory (for this we will use  Plotly, a plotting library with 3D capabilities which we imported at the beginning). \n",
    "\n",
    "The potential energy function that is plotted was calculated by performing single point energy calculations for water in 40 steps ffor all angles between 20 and 170° and distances between 0.6 and 2 Å. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 14,
   "metadata": {
    "editable": true,
    "scrolled": true,
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": ""
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# load in files for PES\n",
    "PES_water = np.load('PES_water/water-hf-6-31G-energies.npy')\n",
    "PES_r = np.load('PES_water/water-hf-6-31G-distances.npy')\n",
    "PES_angle = np.load('PES_water/water-hf-6-31G-angle.npy')\n"
   ]
  },
